    <h2>Roof Orientation</h2>
    <p>South-facing roofs get the most sunlight in the UK and produce the highest output. East and west-facing roofs still work well, generating roughly 15-20% less than south-facing. North-facing roofs are the least ideal, though not always a dealbreaker if the rest of the property is suitable.</p>

    <h2>Roof Angle</h2>
    <p>Most UK roofs are naturally close to the ideal pitch for solar (around 30-40 degrees), so this is rarely a limiting factor. Flat roofs can still work, using angled mounting frames to tilt the panels correctly.</p>

    <h2>Shading</h2>
    <p>Shade from trees, chimneys, or neighbouring buildings can significantly reduce output — even partial shading on one panel can affect the performance of an entire string, depending on the inverter setup. An installer will assess shading patterns throughout the day and across seasons before quoting.</p>

    <h2>Roof Condition and Material</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tiled roofs:</strong> Most common and straightforward to fit.</li>
      <li><strong>Slate roofs:</strong> Slightly more involved to install but well suited to solar.</li>
      <li><strong>Flat roofs:</strong> Workable with mounting frames, though usually slightly more expensive to install.</li>
      <li><strong>Roof age and condition:</strong> If your roof needs replacing within the next 10-15 years, it's often worth doing that first, since removing and refitting panels later adds cost.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Available Roof Space</h2>
    <p>A typical 4kW system needs around 20-24 square metres of usable roof space. Smaller roofs can still work with a smaller system, or with higher-efficiency panels that generate more power per square metre.</p>

    <h2>Planning Permission</h2>
    <p>Most UK homes qualify for "permitted development," meaning no planning permission is needed for standard rooftop solar. Exceptions include listed buildings, some conservation areas, and certain flat installations — an installer can confirm this quickly for your specific property.</p>

    <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
    <p>Most UK homes are suitable for solar in some form — even homes with partial shading, non-ideal orientation, or smaller roofs can often still benefit. The best way to know for certain is a proper site assessment based on your actual roof, not general rules of thumb.</p>

